// FR1 — Golden end-to-end behaviour lock.
//
// This test runs the FULL production pipeline against the committed real
// fixtures and pins its observable output exactly:
//
//	gtfs_static.zip  ->  StaticDB
//	tripupdates.gtfsr -> Poller.parse (LiveStore overlay)
//	QueryArrivals(now = fixtureNow())  ->  []Arrival
//
// The expected tables below were generated by running the pipeline once and
// capturing the actual output verbatim. They are the behaviour lock: any change
// to observable arrivals — route, headsign, scheduled time, whether realtime is
// applied, the delay, or the added flag, in this exact order — fails this test.
// That is precisely what makes an internal refactor (or a performance
// optimisation) safe: if QueryArrivals/parse/buildFromZIPFile still produce
// these arrivals, behaviour is provably unchanged; if they don't, the diff is
// caught here instead of silently shipping to the device.
//
// Everything is deterministic: `now` is pinned to fixtureNow() (the feed's own
// header timestamp, in Europe/Dublin) and the LiveStore clock is pinned via
// withClock, so captured delays/ETAs map to fixed values. To intentionally
// re-baseline (only when behaviour is *meant* to change), re-run the dump logic
// and paste the new tables.

// goldenArrival is the observable projection of an Arrival that this behaviour
// lock pins. ScheduledRel is the scheduled time formatted in Europe/Dublin as
// "15:04"; HasRealtime records whether RealtimeTime was set (a non-zero overlay
// from the realtime feed) without pinning the volatile absolute timestamp.
type goldenArrival struct {
	RouteShort   string
	Headsign     string
	Scheduled    string // "15:04" in Europe/Dublin
	HasRealtime  bool
	DelayMinutes int
	IsAdded      bool
}

